Markdown Explanation for Your Notebook

Methodology

TF-IDF (Term Frequency – Inverse Document Frequency)

TF-IDF converts text comments into numeric features that can be used by a machine learning model.

The process assigns higher importance to words that:

* Occur frequently within a comment (Term Frequency)
* Occur infrequently across all comments (Inverse Document Frequency)

Example:

Comment:
NO COMM METER EXCHANGED
TF-IDF Features:
NO_COMM      0.91
EXCHANGED    0.85
METER        0.04

Common words that appear in many comments receive lower weights, while words that are distinctive to specific comment types receive higher weights.

The result is a numerical representation of each comment called a feature vector.

⸻

Logistic Regression

Logistic Regression is a supervised machine learning classification algorithm.

The model learns relationships between TF-IDF features and manually assigned categories.

For example, it may learn that:

NO_COMM
NO_READ
COMM_FAILURE

are strongly associated with:

NON_COMMUNICATING

while:

EXCHANGE
INSTALL
REMOVE

are associated with:

METER_CHANGE

The model outputs a probability for each category.

Example:

Comment:
NO COMM METER EXCHANGED
Predicted Probabilities:
METER_CHANGE      0.94
NON_COMMUNICATING 0.89
ACCESS_ISSUE      0.01

Categories whose probability exceeds the selected threshold (currently 0.50) are assigned to the comment.

⸻

Interpreting Your Results

Let’s look at the big clue:

Category	Precision	Recall	Support
NON_COMMUNICATING	0.03	1.00	24

This category is screaming at us.

⸻

What Recall = 1.00 Means

It found every actual NON_COMMUNICATING comment.

False Negatives = almost none

Great.

⸻

What Precision = 0.03 Means

This is terrible.

It means:

When the model predicts NON_COMMUNICATING, it’s only correct 3% of the time.

Example:

Model predicts:
100 comments are NON_COMMUNICATING
Reality:
Only 3 actually are.
97 are false alarms.

⸻

Why This Happened

Look at support:

NON_COMMUNICATING = 24

Only 24 examples existed in your test set.

Compare that to:

METER_CHANGE = 982
TAMPERING = 645

Your classes are extremely imbalanced.

The model is basically saying:

“I’m not sure what NON_COMMUNICATING looks like, so I’ll overpredict it.”

⸻

Access Issue

Precision = 1.00
Recall = 0.67
Support = 3

Looks amazing…

Except support is:

3

Three records.

That’s statistically meaningless.

One record changes the score dramatically.

⸻

What I’d Improve First

1. Balance the Categories

This is the biggest issue.

Current:

METER_CHANGE       982
NON_COMMUNICATING   24
ACCESS_ISSUE         3

Ideally:

METER_CHANGE       300
NON_COMMUNICATING  300
ACCESS_ISSUE       300

or at least much closer.

Machine learning hates huge class imbalance.

⸻

2. Review False Positives

Open:

results.to_excel(...)

Filter:

actual_NON_COMMUNICATING = 0
pred_NON_COMMUNICATING = 1

Look at 20-50 examples.

You’ll probably discover:

COMM
RADIO
NETWORK
COLLECTOR

appearing in other categories.

This will tell you exactly why the model is confused.

⸻

3. Look at Important Features

This is my favorite next step.

After training:

clf = model.named_steps["clf"]
tfidf = model.named_steps["tfidf"]
feature_names = tfidf.get_feature_names_out()

For each category:

estimator = clf.estimators_[0]

you can inspect the highest weighted words.

You might discover:

NON_COMMUNICATING
NO_COMM
NO_READ
COMM_FAILURE
NETWORK

or maybe some weird surprises.

This gives explainability.

⸻

4. Tune the Threshold

You’re currently using:

threshold = 0.50

For NON_COMMUNICATING you may want:

threshold = 0.80

This often dramatically improves precision.

Example:

0.50 threshold
Precision = 0.03
0.80 threshold
Precision = 0.60

while sacrificing some recall.

⸻

What Excites Me Most

Your model is already identifying:

METER_CHANGE
TAMPERING
CUSTOMER_OWNED
HARDWARE_FAILURE

with excellent scores.

The biggest issue isn’t TF-IDF or Logistic Regression.

It’s your training data distribution.

Looking at those support values, I’d bet money that if you add:

100-200 more examples

for:

NON_COMMUNICATING
ACCESS_ISSUE

you’ll see the biggest improvement of anything you could do right now.

That’s actually a really encouraging first pass because the model architecture is working; the data just needs to catch up.





